
Data Validity Flags:
====================

  - Goals:

        - Make it impossible to use a non-const reference to the
          System to create inconsistent data.

        - Create validity flags for all data at all levels that
          correctly reflect the state of the data, labelling data
          that has become outdated due to changes in parameters or
          fields.

  - Computed data is stored in multiple places:

       Propagator: q 
       Block:      c and stress, and internal data structures
       Polymer:    phi, mu and Q
       Mixture:    stress
       CFields
       WaveList

    Primary data is stored in:

       WFields
       UnitCell
       Mixture: kuhn
       Block: length or nBead
       Polymer: phi or mu
       Interaction: chi

  - Currently, a non-const reference to a System does not allow 
    non-const access to the mixture, and thus also not to single
    propagators or blocks. It is thus also impossible to use such 
    a handle to update just one propagator, block cField, or block
    stress.

  - Given a non-const reference to a System, the only way to solve the 
    MDE is to call Mixture::compute.  The Mixture::compute function does 
    not take a field or unit cell as an argument, but instead uses the 
    current values stored in WFIelds and Domain::UnitCell.  This makes
    it possible for the System to keep track of when dependent data is
    up to date. No lower level object has access to enough information
    to coordinate this.

  - It is not be necessary for operations that clear flags of objects that 
    are lower in the solver ownership hierarchy (e.g., propgators or blocks)
    to clear flags of higher level aggregates (polymer or mixture). We 
    can rely on the fact that the only way to do updates are globally, 
    by calling member functions of the System or Mixture. Clearing 
    operations only need to go down the ownership hierarchy, not up.

  - The WFields and UnitCell classes do not allow changes to individual
    fields or individual parameters: All fields or all parameters must
    be updated at once. Changes to these data structures can thus be
    treated as unitary events, which trigger emission of signals. 
    Changes in either fields or unit cell parameters invalidates all 
    solutions.

  - Principle: If private or protected data is computed within a class 
    that holds that data, only that class or possibly classes descended
    from it should be able to set the flag to true, and this should be
    done inside the function that does the computation. Such classes 
    should, however, provide public functions to access the flag and 
    to "clear" it (i.e., set it false). The function that clears the 
    flag (marks it as invalid) must be public in order to allow a 
    parent object (Mixture or Polymer) to clear data held by a child 
    (e.g., a Polymer, Block or Propagator). 

  - Following this principle would mean having PropagatorTmpl provide 
    protected access to a function that sets PropagatorTmpl::isSolved_ 
    true, and public access to a function that sets isSolved_ false.

  - Block, Polymer and Mixture should have separate clearUnitCellData 
    and clearWFieldData functions to reflect the need to distinguish 
    between changes in these when resetting private data used in the 
    Block class. 

  - If we continue to use Signals emitted by the UnitCell and WFields 
    objects to clear flags, then resetting both the unit cell and the 
    WFields would force a bit of repetitive flag clearing during SCFT 
    iterations with a flexible unit cell, since either is sufficient 
    to clear all operations. I think that this should be very fast, 
    and is probably okay. 

  - If we start to use signals for changes in other parameters that 
    are changed by a Sweep or a Ramp, flags will be reset multiple 
    times by changes in multiple parameters. Is this okay, or should
    we figure out a way to modify all parameters of a given type at
    once?

  - Reason to worry about this: The change set functions of the System 
    are public. We can only guarantee that they can't be misused if 
    each call guarantees validity.

  - Possible solution: Make the System set functions require that all 
    parameters of one type be reset at once, and then strictly enforce 
    validity on that type of call. 

  - Sweeps and Ramps can accumulate changes in internal variables and 
    then pass all changes of a given type to the System at once. 

Possible changes:
=================

In Pscf::PropagatorTmpl

    Change setIsSolved(bool) to

    protected: setIsSolved(true)  -> public: setIsSolved();
    protected: setIsSolved(false) -> protected: clearSolution();

In Mixture, Polymer and Block:

    Public virtual functions:   


In Block:

    public:

       bool hasCField() const;
       bool hasStress() const;

       void clearWFieldData();
       void clearUnitCellData();

       virtual void setLength();

    private:

       bool hasCField_
       bool hasStress_

    Note: 

    1) The block has different internal data structures that depend 
    on the unit cell parameters and w fields. We thus need different 
    clear functions.

    2) Changing the kuhn length has the same effect on internal data
    structures as changing the unit cell parameters. We don't need a 
    separate clear function for the kuhn length.

    3) Response to changing block lengths can be built into a virtual
    version of the setLength(...) function that calls Edge::setLength(...).

    4) Data used by Block that might be changed in a Sweep or Ramp:

        - W-fields      -> clearWFieldData
        - Kuhn          -> clearUnitCellData
        - Cell params   -> clearUnitCellData
        - Block lengths -> setLength(....)

    5) Multiple clear functions defined in the Block class must be 
    imitated in the Polymer and Mixture class so that they can be called 
    hierarchichally.
         
In Polymer:

    public: 

       void clearWFieldData();
       void clearUnitCellData();

       virtual setPhi(...);
       virtual setMu(...);

In Mixture:

     public:

        bool isSolved() const;
        bool hasStress() const;

     private:

